send.stdout:
`src/lib.rs` has no `--fast` flag to change. `parse_args` currently handles only `--verbose` (a `bool`) and treats everything else as `input`:

```rust
// src/lib.rs:6
pub fn parse_args(args: &[String]) -> Config {
    let mut config = Config { verbose: false, input: String::new() };
    for arg in args {
        match arg.as_str() {
            "--verbose" => config.verbose = true,
            other => config.input = other.to_string(),
        }
    }
    config
}
```

`Config` has only `verbose: bool` and `input: String`; `rg -n "parse_args|fast"` finds no other occurrence anywhere in the repo (`src/lib.rs` is the only source file, and git history is a single `seed` commit). So there is no wrong `bool` type to convert to a `u8` level, and I made no edit rather than inventing a new flag and parsing semantics.

`cargo test` passes unmodified:

```
test tests::parses_verbose ... ok
test result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed
```

If `--fast` is supposed to exist, tell me its intended syntax (`--fast`, `--fast=<level>`, repeated `-f`?) and I will add the field, parsing, and tests.

